ils, or finds the session gone from `Open`, stops staging at once; if a fragment write is then still pending, or the chunk store could not verify one, the error that stops staging is classed indeterminate, carries the renewal's cause and any doubt about its own commit, and is never a plain refusal. No renewal batch can exceed the per-batch operation budget `B_ops`: a knob set whose largest part would need one fails the knob clamps, and staging refuses a request whose chunk bound would. The staged attempt gives the part commit one last renewal of the same kind, which succeeds only when its leases still cover a span the caller names, judged at a clock reading taken after its last commit returned. Every fragment write carries an authorization deadline measured from the clock reading taken before its chunk's intent was committed, and staging waits for the writes no longer than that deadline: a write still pending then, or one the chunk store could not verify, is counted neither written nor not written, the error that stops staging is classed indeterminate in either case, and whatever such a write leaves sits at a position the chunk's owned entry already names. Every body read is bounded too: a read the client leaves without a non-empty piece for the caller's idle bound stops staging, and a piece or end of body that staging only sees once the bound has passed counts as none. An outcome the store could not report is settled by re-reading the batch's own record, never guessed, and an ambiguous reserve retries only the index it may already hold, so no attempt ever holds two slots. Staging writes no `part:` or `psum:` record and releases nothing: it hands its attempt, with one complete chunk reference per chunk, to the part commit (#927), and nothing calls it in production until the S3 `UploadPart` verb is wired (#669).
 
